evals/app/cases/dossier.jsonl:9:{"case_id": "dossier-format-room-handover", "brief": {"answers": {"workingTitle": "Handing Over the Workshop Room", "format": "Numbered procedure", "audience": "New workshop coordinators preparing the room for the next booking", "goal": "Provide an executable sequence for checking tools, clearing tables, and recording unresolved issues.", "tone": "Direct, impersonal, practical", "constraints": "550-700 words; use numbered steps; no first-person anecdotes; every action names its owner.", "successSignal": "A coordinator can follow the procedure without interpreting a personal story."}, "attachments": [], "completedAt": 1790000000000, "updatedAt": 1790000000000}, "draftText": "I first understood the importance of a handover after a long evening in the workshop. I remember feeling pleased with how quickly everyone had left. The evening had been busy and the room finally looked still. I put my bag by the door and began thinking about the journey home. Only then did I notice the materials on the far table. They were not obviously rubbish, and I did not know whether someone expected to find them there in the morning. I stood looking at them for longer than the decision should have required.\n\nThe useful thing about that moment was realizing that a room can look finished without having been handed over. A cleared chair does not tell the next coordinator where the spare parts went. A closed cupboard does not confirm that everything belongs inside it. I began walking around the tables and making a list of questions. The list was longer than I expected. Some questions were about the tools, some about the next booking, and some about decisions I had assumed another person would make before leaving.\n\nThe tools need checking, the tables need clearing, and the remaining materials need recording. On that evening I could see the value of all these actions, but I kept doing them in the order that happened to occur to me. I would cross the room to put something away, notice a different problem, and leave the first task partly complete. The movement felt productive until I realized I had visited the same cupboard repeatedly without finishing the check. There was probably a better sequence, but I was too tired to design it while carrying a tray of small pieces.\n\nI found the handover book beneath a stack of printed instructions. It contained several helpful notes from earlier sessions, though their order reflected the evenings on which people wrote them rather than the order in which a new coordinator would need to act. Reading it made me grateful and a little discouraged. The knowledge was there, but I had to assemble it into a procedure while trying to finish the room. I thought about the person arriving in the morning and wondered which of my own assumptions would become their first problem.\n\nPerhaps someone should tell the next person if anything still needs attention. I wrote a note about the loose handle and put the note beside the book. Then I moved it inside the book because a loose sheet might be missed. Then I wondered whether the maintenance team would ever read that book at all. The note described the problem clearly, but I had not decided who needed to receive it. I could feel myself confusing the act of writing something down with the act of giving it to a person able to do the next part of the work.\n\nBy the time I switched off the lights, I had learned something useful about handovers. They are less about leaving a room looking respectable than about leaving another person enough information to begin. I wished I had understood that before the session ended, when the people who knew the answers were still present. Next time I would begin asking earlier. I would also keep the instructions visible, because remembering a lesson after an awkward evening is not the same as making it available when the room is noisy and everyone wants to leave.\n\nThat is why I think we should take the handover seriously. A few clear decisions can save the next coordinator a great deal of guessing. I hope my experience helps you notice the same small uncertainties before they turn into a delayed start. The room belongs to a sequence of people, each depending on the care of the person before them. Leaving well is part of running the workshop, even when the workshop itself has already ended and the audience has gone home.", "drifts": [{"passage": "I first understood the importance of a handover after a long evening in the workshop.", "issue": "Format and constraints: opens an unnumbered first-person anecdote instead of the required numbered procedure; the rest of the draft continues as a personal narrative."}, {"passage": "The tools need checking, the tables need clearing, and the remaining materials need recording.", "issue": "Constraints and successSignal: passive actions name no owner and provide no executable step order, despite the requirement that each action identify who performs it."}, {"passage": "Perhaps someone should tell the next person if anything still needs attention.", "issue": "Goal and constraints: leaves the handoff optional and ownerless instead of assigning responsibility for recording and communicating unresolved issues."}]}
