 Staff will enter a request in the booking list, confirm that the room is available, and send the confirmation only after the schedule has been updated. If a change becomes necessary, Priya will record what changed and when. The review can then distinguish the experience before and after that change. We should not describe the month as a test of one unchanged procedure if the team has actually used several versions of it.\n\nEach shift will leave a short note about any correction it had to make. The note should describe the problem and the action taken, without including a customer's private message or unrelated personal details. Priya will check the notes during the week and ask for clarification while the event is still easy to recall. This is intended to make the final report understandable, not to rank staff by the number of mistakes they record. A team that reports difficulties clearly should not appear less capable than one whose problems remain invisible.\n\nWe will also record the staff time needed to carry out the procedure. A lower correction count would be useful, but it would not settle whether the additional work is proportionate. At the review, managers should be able to consider the result and the effort together. If staff find that the process adds work without making the schedule clearer, that finding belongs in the report. We should not require the team to describe a frustrating procedure as successful simply because it was introduced with the expectation that it would help.\n\nThe early stopping condition will be handled as a practical safeguard. If a mismatch remains unresolved at the end of a working day, the shift lead will tell Priya and pause new confirmations until the valid schedule is established. Priya will decide whether the trial can resume after the cause is understood and record that decision. Stopping or pausing would not erase the information already collected. It would show that the procedure needs work before the studio can rely on it for another shift.\n\nOn 2 December, Priya will bring the comparison, the staff-time record, and a list of unresolved problems to the managers. The decision will be to keep the procedure, revise it for another bounded test, or return to the previous arrangement. Approving this extension means approving a month of observation with a named owner and a stopping rule. It does not mean approving the final result in advance. That distinction will let the team report what happened honestly and give managers a decision they can explain.", "drifts": []}
evals/app/cases/rewrite.jsonl:7:{"case_id": "rewrite-explainer-jargon-sentence", "personaId": "reader", "level": "sentence", "brief": {"answers": {"workingTitle": "The Night Shift", "format": "science explainer for a weekly popular-science newsletter", "audience": "curious general readers with no biology beyond school", "goal": "explain how the brain clears waste during sleep and what is actually known versus hoped", "tone": "clear, warm, honest about uncertainty", "constraints": "under 900 words; no undefined technical terms; no health advice beyond what the evidence supports", "successSignal": "a reader can explain the glymphatic system to a friend in three sentences"}, "attachments": [], "completedAt": 1790000000000, "updatedAt": 1790000000000}, "draftText": "Every night, while you are doing nothing, your brain runs a cleaning cycle that it cannot run while you are awake. The discovery is barely a decade old, it has already changed how researchers think about sleep, and it begins with a question that sounds like a riddle: how does an organ with no lymphatic vessels take out its rubbish?\n\nThe rest of the body has a drainage network. Lymph vessels run alongside blood vessels, collecting the fluid and waste that leak out of cells and carrying it off to be filtered. The brain, which produces waste faster than almost any other tissue, has no such vessels inside it. For a century this was simply a puzzle, and it remained a puzzle for a long time, as puzzles tend to do when nobody has the tools to solve them. Neurons make junk, the junk goes somewhere, and nobody could say where.\n\nIn 2012 a group at the University of Rochester watched the answer happen in mice. They injected a fluorescent tracer into the cerebrospinal fluid, the clear liquid that bathes the brain, and watched where it went. During sleep the tracer poured into the brain along the outside of arteries, swept through the tissue, and left along the outside of veins, taking dissolved waste with it. During waking it barely moved. The team called the network the glymphatic system, after the glial cells that line its channels, and the name stuck.\n\nThe mechanism is mechanical rather than chemical. When you sleep, the cells of the brain shrink by around sixty percent, opening the spaces between them, and fluid can flow through tissue that was packed tight an hour earlier. The pulse of the arteries pushes it along. Slow-wave sleep, the deep stage that dominates the first half of the night, produces large, slow swells of blood flow, and each swell drives a wave of fluid through the tissue behind it.\n\nWhat gets washed out matters. One of the waste products is beta-amyloid, the protein that clumps into the plaques found in the brains of people with Alzheimer's disease. In the Rochester mice, amyloid cleared about twice as fast during sleep as during waking. In humans, a single night of sleep deprivation raises amyloid in the hippocampus by a measurable amount, which is one of the reasons neurologists have started to take chronic short sleep seriously as a risk factor rather than a lifestyle complaint.\n\nIt is important to be careful here. Mice are not people, tracer studies are indirect, and the human evidence comes mostly from imaging that measures the fluid rather than the waste. The glymphatic system is real in rodents and almost certainly real in us, but \"sleep washes your brain\" is a slogan, not yet a settled finding, and the honest version is that the plumbing exists and the question is how much of the disease it explains.\n\nClearance is modulated by noradrenergic tone, and the perivascular influx is sensitive to the AQP4 polarisation of astrocytic endfeet, which is why the knockout models showed such a striking reduction in tracer penetrance.\n\nStill, the practical lessons are not controversial. Sleep on your side, if you can; in mice, lateral sleeping cleared tracer better than lying on the back or the front, and the reason seems to be gravity and the geometry of the veins in the neck. Protect the first half of the night, because that is when deep sleep happens and deep sleep is when the flow is strongest. Treat a week of five-hour nights as something you did to your brain, not merely something you felt.\n\nThe system also seems to weaken with age. Older mice clear tracer at roughly a third the rate of young ones, and the decline tracks the stiffening of arteries, which push less fluid with each pulse. Nobody has shown the same curve in people, but the stiffening of arteries with age is not in doubt, and the inference is hard to avoid.\n\nWhich leaves the thing that is genuinely strange about all this. For most of the history of biology, sleep looked like an absence, a period in which the interesting processes stopped. It turns out to be a shift change. The crew that runs the day clocks off, and a different crew comes in with the mops, and the only thing they ask is that the building be empty.", "original": "Clearance is modulated by noradrenergic tone, and the perivascular influx is sensitive to the AQP4 polarisation of astrocytic endfeet, which is why the knockout models showed such a striking reduction in tracer penetrance.", "problem": "Five undefined terms in a newsletter for readers with school biology; the rewrite should say the same thing in words a curious non-scientist can follow, keeping the one technical name that matters.", "mustPreserve": ["AQP4"]}
